-- allium: 3
-- agents.allium
--
-- Scope: The Agent — the Claude Code session that works a task — as an
--        entity of its own, separate from the Task it works. Its name, when
--        one starts, when it pauses, when it ends, and how a person or another
--        agent addresses it.
-- Includes: The Agent lifecycle, from assignment at task creation; the agent
--           name, how it is picked and its uniqueness; the task's display
--           name; the one-current-agent rule; the session name an agent
--           is launched under; agent-name resolution wherever a task id is
--           taken.
-- Excludes: The Agent and SubagentEntry entities themselves (core.allium);
--           what a host does to provision a claimed dispatch request — the
--           worktree, the tmux window, the launch (dispatch.allium:
--           ProvisionDispatch); the dispatch claim itself (store-split.allium:
--           RequestDispatch), which this spec extends; the hook rules that
--           write activity (agent-health.allium); the prompt text
--           (dispatch-prompt.allium); the MCP tool contracts
--           (mcp-task-tools.allium); the agent tree's agents section
--           (agent-tree.allium); the subscription mechanics (sync.allium).
-- Dependencies: Task, Host, TmuxWindow, Worktree, Agent, SubagentEntry and the
--               agent enums from core.allium.
--
-- Decisions taken with the user, recorded so they are not re-litigated. Other
-- specs cite them by id.
--   - A1, the entity. An Agent is a stored entity of its own, not a view over
--     Task fields. It owns the facts about the live session; the Task keeps
--     the facts about the work.
--   - A2, one at a time, history kept. A task has at most one current agent.
--     Every fresh launch (dispatch, research, quick, and the relaunch that
--     RetryFresh chains into) starts a NEW agent. A resume keeps the same
--     agent. Ended agents stay as records until their task is deleted.
--   - A3, the name. Every agent gets a person-like first name from a fixed
--     list ("ada", "linus", "grace"). The name is the agent's, not the task's:
--     a task worked by two agents in turn was worked by two people.
--   - A4, a name is held while its agent exists. A name is unique across
--     every Agent row, assigned, current and ended alike. So while a name is
--     held it means one agent, and through it one task. Deleting a task
--     deletes its agents (AgentsGoWithTheirTask), and that frees their names
--     for reuse. When every name in the list is held a number is appended
--     ("ada2"). Changed 2026-11-09 (task #99056): names used to be handed
--     out once ever, through a HandedOutAgentName ledger that kept a deleted
--     task's names taken. With an agent on every task (A17) and feeds
--     creating and deleting tasks often, that used the list up for nothing.
--     The ledger is retired: nothing reads or writes it. Its table stays in
--     the store module only because SpacetimeDB cannot drop a table that
--     holds rows. The cost, accepted: an old log line or message naming a
--     freed name can later point at a different task.
--   - A5, names are fixed. There is no rename. The store picks the name when
--     the agent is assigned or starts, and it never changes.
--   - A6, the name is the task's only second name. Tasks have no alias.
--   - A7, an agent ends when its session can no longer be resumed: a fresh
--     launch replaces it, its worktree goes, or its task is deleted. Closing
--     its window — wrap-up, exit_session, a move back to backlog, a crash —
--     only PAUSES it. A later resume brings back the same agent, under the
--     same name.
--   - A8, addressing. Wherever a tool takes a task id, it also takes an agent
--     name, and the name resolves to that agent's task.
--   - A9, the session name. An agent is launched with its own name as the
--     Claude Code session name (`--name ada`), so ListAgents and SendMessage
--     address it by name. The tmux WINDOW keeps its `task-<id>` name: the
--     window belongs to the task's worktree and the board finds it by task.
--   - A10, one epic. The agent model was built under one epic of its own, one
--     task per step.
--   - A11, the name list. Over six hundred short first names (three to seven
--     lowercase ASCII letters) from many cultures, easy to read and say. The
--     list leaves out names that are also common English words or tool names
--     (will, mark, max, june, rose, ruby, julia, jack, perl and the like). It
--     keeps ada, linus and grace. Append-only: a handed-out name is never
--     recomputed, so editing the list changes only names not yet picked.
--   - A12, a launch that never got a worktree ends its agent. The agent is
--     created at the claim, before the host has made a worktree. When that
--     request fails and the claim is released (store-split.allium:
--     ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest), the agent ends with end reason
--     launch_failed (EndAgentWhenLaunchFails). It is ended, not deleted: it
--     was handed a name, and the ledger keeps that name either way.
--   - A13, where Agent lives. The Agent entity is in core.allium, because
--     SubagentEntry.agent and the Task derivations is_detached and
--     is_unprovisioned refer to it. Its rules are here.
--   - A14, SubStatusOwner. Task.sub_status is one field on the Task. The
--     session half of it (active, idle, needs_input, crashed) is not on the
--     Agent. The hook rules write it on the Task, deciding it from the current
--     agent's facts.
--   - A15, a hook for a task with no current agent. A task in running or
--     review always has one except while unprovisioned (never launched). A
--     hook that arrives for a task with none writes no agent fact and starts
--     none; the Task-side write (sub_status, status) happens as usual.
--   - A16, an agent that ends takes its SubagentEntry rows with it
--     (EndedAgentLetsGoOfItsSession), so every entry belongs to a current
--     agent.
--   - A17, every task gets an agent when it is created (task #99056). Task
--     ids grow in jumps (the store reserves them in blocks and drops the
--     rest of a block on restart), so ids make poor names for people. The
--     agent is created in state `assigned`, in the same write as the task,
--     on every create path: create_task, quick dispatch, a feed's insert, a
--     phoenix respawn. Not on a seed or an import, which restore rows with
--     their own ids and bring their own agents. The task's first dispatch
--     claim launches the assigned agent: it becomes current under the same
--     name. A later fresh launch still starts a new agent (A2).
--   - A18, the display name. A task is shown to people by the name of its
--     assigned or current agent, else of the agent that ended last
--     (core.allium: Task.display_name). A task moved back to backlog keeps
--     showing its last agent's name until a fresh launch starts a new one.
--     The id stays the key every tool takes; the display name is what a
--     person reads.

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./store-split.allium" as store_split
use "./dispatch.allium" as dispatch

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A new task gets its agent at once (A17). The agent is assigned: it has a
-- name and has never run.
rule AssignAgentOnTaskCreated {
    when: task: core/Task.created

    -- A seed or import restores a row with its own id and brings that row's
    -- agents with it (spacetime-seed.allium); it is not a creation here.
    requires: not is_restored(task)

    let name = fresh_agent_name()

    ensures: core/Agent.created(
        task: task,
        name: name,
        state: assigned,
        live_subagents: 0,
        stop_pending: false
    )

    @guidance
        -- The store creates it in the one task write
        -- (spacetime/module/src/tasks_epics.rs::store_task), on the insert
        -- branch with id 0: every reducer that creates a task goes through
        -- it, feed inserts included. Seeds keep their explicit id and do not.
        -- `probe_generated_task_id` creates and then removes a probe task, so
        -- it assigns and frees one name per restore check; harmless.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- is_restored(task): the row was written with an explicit id by a
        -- seed or import, not generated by the store.
}

-- A fresh launch launches the task's assigned agent if it has one (A17), and
-- otherwise starts a new agent and ends the one it replaces.
--
-- The agent is created inside the dispatch claim (store-split.allium:
-- RequestDispatch), in the same transaction that moves the task to running.
-- That is where the store resets the session facts, and it is the only place
-- the name can be picked before the host launches: the host reads the agent's
-- name to build `--name` (AgentSessionIsNamedAfterTheAgent).
--
-- A dispatch that reuses a worktree kept by MoveTaskBackward still starts a
-- new agent. It launches a fresh Claude session, not a resume, so it is a new
-- person picking up the work.
rule StartAgentOnFreshLaunch {
    -- Reported unreachable until store-split.allium declares the module
    -- reducer surface that provides DispatchRequested.
    when: store_split/DispatchRequested(task, requester, origin, mode?)

    -- Fires only when the claim succeeds (every requires of
    -- store-split.allium: RequestDispatch holds).

    let previous = current_agent(task)
    let waiting = assigned_agent(task)
    -- The mode RequestDispatch derives (its `chosen_mode`): the caller's when
    -- it names one, else research for an unplanned research-tagged task and
    -- dispatch otherwise. A `quick` request starts a `quick` agent.
    let start_mode =
        if mode != null: mode
        else: if task.has_plan or task.tag != research: dispatch else: research

    ensures:
        if exists previous:
            previous.state = ended
            previous.ended_at = now
            previous.end_reason = replaced
    ensures:
        if exists waiting:
            -- The agent picked at creation runs the work, under its name.
            waiting.state = current
            waiting.host = requester
            waiting.start_mode = start_mode
            waiting.started_at = now
            waiting.tmux_window = null
            waiting.last_pre_tool_use_at = now
        else:
            core/Agent.created(
                task: task,
                name: fresh_agent_name(),
                host: requester,
                start_mode: start_mode,
                state: current,
                started_at: now,
                tmux_window: null,
                last_pre_tool_use_at: now,
                live_subagents: 0,
                stop_pending: false
            )

    @guidance
        -- The new agent starts without a window and turns live when
        -- ProvisionDispatch records one for it (HostReportsDispatched). The
        -- claim stamps last_pre_tool_use_at on it, so the dispatch watchdog
        -- measures from the claim (agent-health.allium). `current_agent(task)`
        -- is the task's one agent with state = current, or null
        -- (OneCurrentAgentPerTask).
        --
        -- Every claim gets an agent, whichever path wrote the request: the
        -- request_dispatch reducer and the epic chain inside
        -- request_exit_session share one write
        -- (spacetime/module/src/dispatch_request.rs::create_dispatch_request),
        -- and the agent is created or launched there. The claim's own task
        -- write runs first, so a refused claim touches no agent and picks no
        -- name.
        --
        -- A task has an assigned agent only until its first claim: a task
        -- that has launched once has a current or ended agent instead, so a
        -- later fresh launch (RetryFresh, a dispatch after a move back) takes
        -- the else branch and starts a new agent (A2). A task with no agent
        -- at all (an old row the backfill has not reached) also takes it.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- assigned_agent(task) is the task's one agent with state = assigned,
        -- or null (OneOpenAgentPerTask).
        --
        -- fresh_agent_name() is the picker
        -- (spacetime/module/src/agent.rs::pick_agent_name). It starts at a
        -- clock-seeded place in the name list (A11) and walks forward to the
        -- first name no Agent row holds. When every name is held it appends
        -- 2, 3, ... to the name at the seed (A4).
}

-- A launch whose request fails before any worktree exists ends its agent
-- (A12). Fires with ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest, in the same write, and only
-- when that rule's release applies: the task is still the bare claim
-- (running, no worktree). A request that fails after its task got a worktree
-- (a dispatch that reused a kept one) leaves the agent current, because the
-- worktree it works in is still there.
rule EndAgentWhenLaunchFails {
    when: store_split/FailedDispatchReleasedClaim(task)

    let agent = current_agent(task)

    requires: exists agent

    ensures: agent.state = ended
    ensures: agent.ended_at = now
    ensures: agent.end_reason = launch_failed

    @guidance
        -- spacetime/module/src/dispatch_request.rs calls
        -- end_current_agent with AGENT_END_LAUNCH_FAILED inside the release.
}

-- An agent whose worktree goes can never be resumed, so it ends. RetryFresh
-- is the usual cause: it releases the worktree, and the agent ends before the
-- relaunch RetryFresh chains into starts the next one.
rule EndAgentWhenWorktreeGoes {
    when: task: core/Task.worktree transitions_to null

    let agent = current_agent(task)

    requires: exists agent

    ensures: agent.state = ended
    ensures: agent.ended_at = now
    ensures: agent.end_reason = worktree_gone

    @guidance
        -- The store ends the agent on the task write that clears the
        -- worktree, whichever reducer carries it
        -- (spacetime/module/src/agent.rs::follow_task_write). In RetryFresh
        -- that is the board's Persist of the task moved back to backlog
        -- (src/runtime/tasks.rs::exec_persist_task), which clears the
        -- worktree because RetryFresh says to (PersistFields::release_worktree);
        -- the later pointer clear
        -- (src/runtime/tasks.rs::clear_worktree_pointer) then finds nothing to
        -- end. Keying it on the write rather than on RetryFresh keeps
        -- CurrentAgentTracksWorktree true whichever write clears it.
        --
        -- No other Persist clears the worktree
        -- (tasks.allium: ABoardWriteLeavesThePointersAlone): a stale board
        -- copy that lacks the worktree cannot end the agent.
}

-- An agent that ends lets go of its session in the same write: its window,
-- its subagent entries and any deferred Stop (A16). This is the one place
-- that clearing happens for every end reason, so core.allium's
-- EndedAgentHoldsNothing holds by construction.
rule EndedAgentLetsGoOfItsSession {
    when: agent: core/Agent.state transitions_to ended

    ensures:
        agent.tmux_window = null
        agent.live_subagents = 0
        agent.stop_pending = false
        for entry in core/SubagentEntries where agent = agent:
            not exists entry

    @guidance
        -- One function writes every end
        -- (spacetime/module/src/agent.rs::end_current_agent): it stores
        -- ended_agent's row and deletes the task's subagent entries. The
        -- hook timestamps stay on the ended row, as history nothing reads.
}

-- Every task with a worktree and no current agent gets one, with a fresh
-- name. The repair keeps CurrentAgentTracksWorktree true for rows that were
-- written without an agent. Every task with no agent at all, and no
-- worktree, gets an assigned one, so every task has a display name (A17,
-- A18). Idempotent: a second run finds nothing to do.
rule BackfillCurrentAgents {
    when: AgentBackfillRequested()

    ensures:
        for t in core/Tasks where t.worktree != null and not exists current_agent(t):
            core/Agent.created(
                task: t,
                name: fresh_agent_name(),
                host: t.host,
                start_mode: dispatch,
                state: current,
                started_at: now
            )
    ensures:
        for t in core/Tasks where t.worktree = null and (core/Agents where task = t).count = 0:
            core/Agent.created(
                task: t,
                name: fresh_agent_name(),
                state: assigned,
                live_subagents: 0,
                stop_pending: false
            )

    @guidance
        -- A stored Task row may still hold the six session facts in its
        -- legacy columns. The same pass copies them onto the task's current
        -- agent, points the task's SubagentEntry rows at it, and blanks them
        -- on the Task, so a later run finds nothing to copy and cannot
        -- overwrite a newer agent fact with an older Task one. A task with no
        -- current agent whose row still holds facts has them blanked and its
        -- entries dropped
        -- (spacetime/module/src/agent.rs::backfill_agents_into).
        --
        -- The start mode is `dispatch` because the store cannot tell how the
        -- old session started, and dispatch is the common case.
}

-- A dispatched session is named after its agent.
--
-- ProvisionDispatch and every resume pass the current agent's name as
-- Claude Code's `--name` (src/dispatch/agents.rs::session_name_flag). The tmux
-- window keeps its `task-<id>` name.
rule AgentSessionIsNamedAfterTheAgent {
    when: dispatch/AgentLaunched(task, mode)

    let agent = current_agent(task)

    requires: exists agent

    ensures: LaunchedWithSessionName(task: task, session_name: agent.name)
}

-- An agent name resolves to its task wherever a tool takes a task id.
--
-- The argument is read as an id when it is all digits. Anything else is looked
-- up as an agent name, assigned, current or ended, and resolves to that
-- agent's task. A
-- name that names no agent is the same "not found" an unknown id is.
rule AgentNameResolvesToTask {
    when: McpToolCalled(task_ref)

    requires: not is_all_digits(task_ref)

    let agent = core/Agent{name: task_ref}

    ensures:
        if exists agent:
            ResolvedTo(task: agent.task)
        else:
            NotFound(reference: task_ref)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What an MCP client sees of a task's agent, and how it names one. `state`
-- shows as live, paused or ended.
surface AgentIdentity {
    facing _: core/McpClient

    context task: core/Task

    exposes:
        -- get_task and list_tasks show the task's display name (A18) and
        -- the state of the agent it comes from: live, paused, not started
        -- (assigned) or ended.
        task.display_name
        display_agent(task).state
        display_agent(task).is_live

    provides:
        -- Every MCP tool that takes a task id (mcp-task-tools.allium:
        -- TaskRefResolvesToTaskId).
        McpToolCalled(task_ref)

    @guarantee ResumeKeepsTheAgent
        -- A resume continues the current agent and never starts one, so the
        -- name a client saw before the resume is the name after it (A2, A7).
        -- Only AssignAgentOnTaskCreated, StartAgentOnFreshLaunch and
        -- BackfillCurrentAgents create an agent, and a resume (dispatch.allium: ResumeTask, RetryResume)
        -- emits no DispatchRequested. The resume's window write lands on the
        -- current agent's window: that write is what brings the agent back.
        -- A task with no current agent has nothing to resume into;
        -- ResumeTask's own worktree requirement refuses that case
        -- (CurrentAgentTracksWorktree).

    @guarantee PromptNamesTheAgent
        -- A dispatched agent's prompt tells it its own name (a line in the
        -- task block, for every mode). That other agents are reached by their
        -- agent names, which each task's details and listing show, is stated
        -- once for every session by the MCP server instructions
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: ServerInstructionsStateDispatchConventions),
        -- not in each prompt. Nothing says siblings run as `task-<id>`
        -- (dispatch-prompt.allium).
}

-- How tasks and epics are named to people (A18, task #99056). Ids stay the
-- key every tool takes and every row is stored under; they are poor names
-- for people because they grow in jumps. So what a person reads names a task
-- by its display name and an epic by its title.
surface NamesForPeople {
    facing _: core/Operator

    context task: core/Task
    context epic: core/Epic

    exposes:
        task.display_name
        task.title
        epic.title

    @guarantee BoardMessagesNameTasksAndEpics
        -- Every status line, error popup, notice and desktop notification
        -- the board or the agent tree shows names a task by its display
        -- name, or by its title when it has none yet, and an epic by its
        -- title. None shows a bare `#<id>` or `task <id>`: "Bookmarked
        -- nevena2", not "Bookmarked #99056"; "Auto-dispatch of nevena2
        -- failed — epic Dispatch stalled", not "#99056 … epic #314". The
        -- unseen-items admin view is the one exception: it shows ids and no
        -- titles on purpose (admin-surfaces.allium). Exact wording is each
        -- message's own spec.

    @guarantee McpRepliesNameWhatTheyTouch
        -- An MCP reply that names a task keeps its id, as the key, and adds
        -- the task's display name and title beside it; one that names an
        -- epic adds the epic's title. That covers the replies that today
        -- give a bare id — create_task, update_tasks (one, several, done),
        -- update_epic, dispatch_task, resume_task, wrap_up, exit_session,
        -- and the work-package replies and their item lists — and the rows
        -- that name a parent by id alone (list_epics' `(parent:<id>)`).
        -- Work-package task items show their display name. The exact reply
        -- strings are in mcp-task-tools.allium, epics.allium and
        -- work-packages.allium. Epic titles are not unique, and a tool never
        -- resolves an epic by title: an agent that is given an ambiguous
        -- title asks which epic was meant.
}

-- The shared store runs the agent backfill on its own: on the first
-- connection after a publish that changed its rules version, where it
-- re-stamps that version
-- (spacetime/module/src/lib.rs::stamp_rules_version_on_connect), so an
-- upgraded store is backfilled before any board reads it. `store import`
-- also asks for it, through the backfill_agents reducer, once its rows are
-- written (spacetime-seed.allium: ImportOldStore).
surface AgentBackfill {
    facing _: core/SharedStore

    provides:
        AgentBackfillRequested()
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- At most one current agent per task (A2).
invariant OneCurrentAgentPerTask {
    for t in core/Tasks:
        (core/Agents where task = t and is_current).count <= 1
}

-- At most one agent per task that is not ended: assigned and current never
-- sit side by side. The first claim turns the assigned agent current
-- (StartAgentOnFreshLaunch), and nothing assigns an agent to a task that
-- already has one (AssignAgentOnTaskCreated fires on creation only; the
-- backfill assigns only to a task with no agents at all).
invariant OneOpenAgentPerTask {
    for t in core/Tasks:
        (core/Agents where task = t and state != ended).count <= 1
}

-- An assigned agent has never launched, so its task has no worktree and the
-- agent holds no session facts.
invariant AssignedAgentHasNoSession {
    for a in core/Agents where is_assigned:
        a.task.worktree = null and a.tmux_window = null
            and a.live_subagents = 0 and a.stop_pending = false
}

-- A task has a current agent if and only if it has a worktree, except while
-- a fresh launch is in flight.
--
-- One way round: a session needs a directory to come back to. The other way
-- round is load-bearing too: a resume and AgentSessionIsNamedAfterTheAgent
-- both need a current agent, so a task with a worktree and no agent could be
-- neither resumed nor launched. The dispatch claim (StartAgentOnFreshLaunch)
-- and the backfill (BackfillCurrentAgents) create the agent;
-- EndAgentWhenWorktreeGoes ends it in the same write that clears the
-- worktree.
--
-- The exception (A12): the claim creates the agent before the host has made
-- the worktree, so a task whose dispatch request is still open may hold a
-- current agent and no worktree yet. The request then either records the
-- worktree (HostReportsDispatched) or fails and ends the agent
-- (EndAgentWhenLaunchFails).
invariant CurrentAgentTracksWorktree {
    for t in core/Tasks:
        (t.worktree != null implies exists current_agent(t))
        and (exists current_agent(t) implies
                (t.worktree != null or has_open_dispatch_request(t)))
}

-- An agent goes with its task. Removing a task removes every agent it has
-- had, assigned, current and ended, whatever the task's status. That frees
-- their names (A4).
--
-- Every path that removes a task row removes its agents: DeleteTask,
-- BatchDelete, an epic delete's subtree and a feed's stale-task sweep. They
-- share one list of side effects
-- (spacetime/module/src/tasks_epics.rs::delete_task_side_effects), and the
-- agents go there.
invariant AgentsGoWithTheirTask {
    for a in core/Agents:
        exists a.task
}

-- No two agents hold the same name (A4), whatever their state. A name held
-- by an ended agent stays held until its task is deleted.
invariant AgentNamesAreUnique {
    for x in core/Agents: for y in core/Agents:
        x.name = y.name implies x = y
}

-- Every task has an agent from the moment it is created (A17), so it always
-- has a display name (A18). Tasks restored by a seed bring theirs, and the
-- backfill covers rows written before A17.
invariant EveryTaskHasAnAgent {
    for t in core/Tasks:
        (core/Agents where task = t).count >= 1
}

-- An agent's name is never only digits, so it can never be read as a task id.
invariant AgentNameIsNotAnId {
    for a in core/Agents:
        not is_all_digits(a.name)
}
